## The private way down the Nile

Between Luxor and Aswan lie three temples that most visitors see from a cruise ship, arriving with the whole boat and leaving on the boat's timetable. The Temple Road is the other way to do it. Your own car, your bags in the boot, your concierge beside you, and a day planned around one idea: arrive at each temple when it's calm.

You leave Luxor at about seven, after breakfast. You arrive in Aswan in the afternoon, in good time to watch the sun go down over the river.

## Three temples, three different wonders

**Esna** comes first, about an hour south: a hall of twenty-four columns sunk nine metres below a market town, its ceiling cleaned back to colour by restorers in 2023. **Edfu**, further on, is the Temple of Horus, roofed from gate to sanctuary, with its granite falcon at the door and its shrine still in shadow. **Kom Ombo**, last, is the double temple of Sobek and Horus the Elder, standing above a bend of the river, with the Crocodile Museum beside it.

> The cruise ships take days to see these temples. You see them in one, at a calm moment, with your bags in the boot.

## The road between

The drive is part of the pleasure. The road follows the Nile through palm groves, cane fields and mud-brick villages, with the desert hills never far away, and there is always somewhere good to stop for coffee or a proper lunch. On the Original day, your concierge holds the day and gives you time at each temple; on the Signature day, a licensed Egyptologist can travel with you and read all three.

## Left to us

This is a one-way day, ending in a different city, so it carries a €150 road supplement for the car. We never drive the round trip by road in a single day: it's too far to do safely in daylight. The order, the timing and every arrangement on the road are left to your concierge, with local knowledge and our craft, and every road day is planned to arrive before dusk. It works just as well from Aswan to Luxor. Tell us which way you're travelling, and we'll take care of everything else.
